          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-bbe05d10a5783b37\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"Ring’s “jeg elsker dig, Constance” is principally an emphatic avowal that presents his love as true. Its attempt to preserve the marriage and obtain forgiveness gives it weak undertaking-related force, but the commitment is conveyed more clearly by the surrounding promises and pleas than by the love utterance itself. His crying, sudden alarm, and convulsive delivery establish emotional intensity, not an independently expressive/reflex-like core force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The sincerity of the avowal is doubtful in light of Ring’s self-interest and dishonesty, but sincerity does not affect its truth-conditional mode. There is limited ambiguity over whether the utterance itself helps renew his marital commitment or merely supports his plea for reconciliation.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Ring directly addresses his wife Constance, crying out that he loves her and immediately expanding the avowal into “I love and adore you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Constance is threatening to withdraw from Ring after discovering his involvement with her servant. Terrified of losing her, Ring uses the avowal within an extravagant performance of abasement, denial, and pleading for forgiveness. The wider passage makes the appeal self-serving and potentially manipulative: he has promised not to abandon the servant, minimizes what happened, and the narration indicates that Constance does not know the full truth. Nevertheless, the highlighted words conventionally function as an avowal of love rather than as a merely instrumental token. Constance initially dismisses them as nonsense, showing her distrust rather than changing their core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“men jeg elsker dig, Constance” (“but I love you, Constance”).\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration directly presents Ring’s loving state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ring continues, “jeg elsker og forguder dig” and says he cares for no one in the world but her.\",\"supports\":\"The elaboration reinforces the utterance as an avowal of love and adoration rather than a semantically empty use.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before speaking, Ring experiences terror at losing Constance; afterward he begs for forgiveness and asks her to receive him back into favor.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal serves a reconciliation attempt and therefore has weak performative or undertaking-related support, while its principal role remains assertional.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He cries the speech, holds his head, writhes as if in convulsions, and later weeps.\",\"supports\":\"These details establish passionate delivery but, without evidence that the words are a reflex-like discharge rather than an intense avowal and plea, do not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ring has promised not to abandon Alette, denies being in a relationship with her, and the narration later indicates that Constance lacks the experience to suspect the truth.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs amid concealment and self-interested persuasion, creating doubt about sincerity without altering its T/P/E mode.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense, main-or-embedded valid explicit “I love you” construction in Amalie Skram’s Constance Ring.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the targeted occurrence and its explicit love-avowal form.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the central avowal, with weak P support for its role in seeking relational restoration. The surrounding manipulation, possible insincerity, and emotional intensity are contextual features adequately described without O or an independent E score.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
